WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.650
- My next guest is a very
funny man whom you've seen

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.490
in New Girl and The Neighborhood.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.800
He's also the father of
two children, a credit

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.070
that inspired him to create DADZ.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.410
It's the first dad-focused
parenting and baby goods company.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.870
Let's say hi to the truly
talented Max Greenfield.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.120
What's up, Max!

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:16.953
- Hey!

00:00:16.953 --> 00:00:19.060
- I love your quarantine stache.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:20.784
- It's a whole thing.

00:00:20.784 --> 00:00:22.400
(laughs)

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.100
- I love it, did you try the full beard

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.830
or you just went for Tom Selleck?

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.207
- I went beard at first
and then they were like,

00:00:28.207 --> 00:00:29.980
"Don't touch your face."

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.980
And (laughs) then I was itching
the beard and I was like,

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:37.480
"I gotta shave this off
so I don't get sick."

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.660
And then I was like, "Well
I shouldn't shave it all."

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.190
And then, now I just have a mustache.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.570
- It's awesome, look
CHiPs is gonna be calling.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:45.700
I'm into it.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.330
Well your kids are 10 and four now,

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:49.230
so how has it been locked inside with them

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.803
for the last four months?

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.895
- We've really gotten to know each other.

00:00:54.895 --> 00:00:57.340
(laughs)

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:58.990
- Probably about as good as mine.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.060
I love how honest you are.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.620
And it's crazy though, right?

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:06.730
I feel like, here's the thing,
I started therapy up again

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:08.617
in quarantine because I
was like, "I need someone

00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:12.010
"to talk to outside of this
house and I will pay them."

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.597
So I literally was telling
my therapist, like,

00:01:14.597 --> 00:01:19.597
"I feel like it's almost
like two different humans

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.190
in one body."

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.900
It'll be so great and then all the sudden

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.230
it's just like demons
are just coming out at me

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.110
and I don't know what is happening.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.153
- Yeah, I don't know.

00:01:31.153 --> 00:01:35.540
Because my daughter's
10 and my son is four

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.430
and there's this thing, that's
a pretty significant age gap,

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.440
so there's not a lot
of things or activities

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.310
- Oh, to do together.
- to do together.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:44.180
- Yeah.
- So a lot

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:48.080
of it is like keeping one at bay,

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.710
and then really focusing on one,

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.430
and then going back and forth,

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:53.380
- Yeah.
- and then you're sort of

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.950
caught off guard when you're with one

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.270
and it's going really well,
like the homeschooling

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.980
with my daughter, there
are days, most days,

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.440
I think it goes really well

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:03.837
and I'm like "Ooh, we're in a good zone.

00:02:03.837 --> 00:02:05.320
"She's reading and we're doing this."

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.210
And then I'll go to my son

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.700
and be like "All right, you're
good, you're doing this."

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.039
I go over to my son and
my son's like (groans),

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:12.757
and I go "No, we just had a good vibe.

00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:15.230
"Why are you gonna ruin
the vibe over here?"

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:17.950
- All right, well everybody
at home, Max homeschooled

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.180
his daughter through
the end of fourth grade.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:21.130
No easy task.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:22.800
He recorded some of those lessons

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:23.830
and made a little mash up.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:24.663
Can we play that?

00:02:24.663 --> 00:02:25.650
Because it's awesome.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.883
- So how do you find
the area of the square?

00:02:31.056 --> 00:02:32.165
- So basically.

00:02:32.165 --> 00:02:34.205
- Okay, so how do you find the perimeter?

00:02:34.205 --> 00:02:35.193
- So basically.

00:02:35.193 --> 00:02:37.900
- How do you find the perimeter
of an irregular shape?

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.510
- So basically.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.250
- Who fought the French-Indian War?

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.142
- So basically the Indians.

00:02:44.142 --> 00:02:45.684
♪ Ain't trying to be cool like you ♪

00:02:45.684 --> 00:02:47.458
♪ Wobblin' around in
your high heel shoes ♪

00:02:47.458 --> 00:02:48.655
♪ I'm clumsy ♪

00:02:48.655 --> 00:02:50.560
- In a book, what's the glossary?

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.410
- So basically, it's like the dictionary

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:54.583
but like much smaller.

00:02:57.359 --> 00:02:58.192
Okay.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.183
Hi.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:03.173
Whoa!

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.320
- How would you describe this hairstyle?

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.853
- So basically, it's like
a dog because I love dogs.

00:03:12.963 --> 00:03:15.550
(laughs)

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:18.180
- Okay, so basically she got it,

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:19.990
and you just didn't understand, I guess.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:22.317
So basically, (laughs) it was awesome.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.130
Like does she always
start sentences like that?

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.575
- Yeah, she does and then we did the video

00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:33.575
and it was really funny
and people have responded

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:35.940
how funny they thought it was,

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:40.107
and I think she thought "Oh,
I should like up the amount

00:03:40.107 --> 00:03:42.397
"of times that I'm saying so basically."

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.830
It sort of reaffirmed
and gave her confidence

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:50.763
in that phrase, and I,
since, had to be like "Hey,

00:03:52.157 --> 00:03:55.160
"so we don't wanna start
every sentence with that."

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.370
- Yeah, well see maybe a few.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.203
(laughs)

00:03:58.203 --> 00:03:59.390
- Yeah, and I don't wanna discourage her

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:00.500
and make her feel bad about it,

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.800
- Yeah.
- but there was a gentle sort

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.497
of like "Let's find a different end

00:04:05.497 --> 00:04:08.150
"to when we're just talking
about, I don't know, anything."

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.920
- Yeah, well on a serious note,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.080
what did you learn from
teaching fourth grade

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.920
for a couple of months?

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.833
- Oh my god, patience.

00:04:16.833 --> 00:04:17.818
Really.
(laughs)

00:04:17.818 --> 00:04:19.973
It was a real exercise in patience.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.880
- Do you ever find
yourself doing the homework

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.987
and going, "I don't know how to do this

00:04:24.987 --> 00:04:28.010
"and I'm a grown woman."
- So much of it.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.660
So much of it, I can't even tell you.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.890
There was like, I got to a point where,

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.390
because the beginning was
just completely overwhelming,

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:37.370
and then you get into a groove

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:38.840
and you sort of start realizing.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:42.563
Okay, so there's this math assignment.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.880
They're like tessellations.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.400
I've never even heard about tessellations,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.230
so I told Lily that she's
probably never gonna hear

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:51.662
of one, either--

00:04:51.662 --> 00:04:53.853
- Probably never gonna use this.

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:56.193
- No one will use this.

00:04:56.193 --> 00:04:57.026
(laughs)
But then some

00:04:57.026 --> 00:04:59.760
of the other stuff, like the English

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.030
and storytelling and some

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:03.420
of the writing assignments,
we really focused on

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:04.980
and I think we did a good job.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.526
There were some real wins in this.

00:05:07.526 --> 00:05:09.310
- And you gotta celebrate those.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:10.490
- Yeah.
- Well what about your son?

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.260
Did he learn anything?
Did you do any Zoom stuff

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:13.210
with him?
- No.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:14.043
- No.

00:05:14.043 --> 00:05:15.745
(laughs)

00:05:15.745 --> 00:05:17.478
- You just can focus on the one.

00:05:17.478 --> 00:05:18.540
(laughs)

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.450
- He's four and a half.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.310
They'll be some significant damage to him

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:26.330
and we have time to make that up.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:29.580
Whereas Lily's 10 and she's
really on the precipice

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.030
of some important stuff
and if she gets to,

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:35.760
she can get left behind right now,

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.450
and we're trying to avoid that.

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:40.610
So my son, we'll figure him out.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:42.510
- Well, speaking of being
a dad though, tell me

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.700
about this new company you founded, DADZ.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.613
- Yeah, we're really excited about it.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.260
Honestly, I don't know that I'm
ever gonna be doing anything

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:53.130
other than hanging out with these kids,

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:55.290
and trying to take care of them,

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.793
so we decided to start
a business out of it.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:03.310
But it really started
a communication, a lot

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.330
of this has started a communication

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.170
between me and a lot of other dads,

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:11.770
and just reaching out with support

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.903
and laughter, and sharing
a lot of these moments,

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.320
and I talked to these guys

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:23.710
who had started this, I
had this idea for DADZ

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.030
and I jumped right in.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:26.270
I loved what they were about.

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:28.930
Supporting dads and
supporting this community,

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:32.303
and just actively trying
to get dads more involved,

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:38.390
and really just having a product line

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:41.350
for dads that supports one another and--

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:44.130
- It's crazy that it hasn't
really happened before.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.400
- I know, and that's what
we have been talking about,

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:47.710
and that's what we're trying to do,

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:52.710
and we have a charitable
aspect to our company

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:56.280
and a lot of our proceeds will go

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.180
to a revolving door of
different father charities.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:01.608
The one we're working with right now

00:07:01.608 --> 00:07:05.340
is called Fathers Incorporated
that's based in Atlanta,

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:10.291
and this is just a real positive company,

00:07:10.291 --> 00:07:13.790
and I'm so excited to be involved.

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:15.070
- That's so cool.

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:17.900
It's a great, innovative idea.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:19.620
I wanna show the photo though

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:20.830
from your press release for DADZ

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:22.893
because it's so awesome.
- Yeah.

00:07:22.893 --> 00:07:23.726
(laughs)

00:07:23.726 --> 00:07:26.380
- And such a great example
of what really happens.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:27.670
I love that most people, you know,

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:30.300
they make their Instagrams
like a fake life,

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.000
like a fake version of their life.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.397
Yours is like real.

00:07:33.397 --> 00:07:35.920
(laughs) And that's what's so awesome,

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.250
because are they really,
is this a real reaction?

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.730
Is he really crying?

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:41.590
- Yeah, this is what happens if I try

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:44.380
to get everyone in the picture.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.260
- Well man, thank you
so much for joining us.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:47.593
You are hysterical.

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:49.570
I love you.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:51.690
Every time you come here,
everybody, we were all talking

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:53.426
about it before you logged in.

00:07:53.426 --> 00:07:54.259
- Oh, you're the best.

00:07:54.259 --> 00:07:55.092
I love coming on.

00:07:55.092 --> 00:07:56.050
This has been so great.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:56.883
- Yes.
- Thanks for having me.

00:07:56.883 --> 00:07:59.640
- And good luck to your family
and all of our families.

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:01.870
We're all stuck at home and
this has been really hard

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.760
and, relatively speaking, for
everyone in their own world

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:07.650
so thank you so much for joining us

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:09.775
and Max thanks so much.

00:08:09.775 --> 00:08:11.020
Tell your kiddos thank you

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:12.530
because that made me laugh so hard.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:14.750
Well, DADZ is rolling out
new products all the time.

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:17.390
Check out their website
for more info, everybody.

